## A valley that wakes before the town does

Mariánské Lázně — Marienbad in German, and still widely known by that name — sits in a narrow valley enclosed by forest on three sides. That is why spring shows itself here first among the trees rather than on the promenade. Along the damp margins of the streams the white bells of spring snowflakes appear, hollow corydalis flowers across the beech woodland floor before the canopy closes, and the air suddenly smells different: of wet earth and opening leaves. **Spring in Mariánské Lázně** is the season when the park and the colonnade get their colour back — and when the town is still empty enough to feel like your own.

Where summer brings festivals and crowds, a spring day here moves slowly. A treatment in the morning, a long walk through the park after lunch, coffee on the colonnade in the afternoon, and an evening quiet you will not find in July. Anyone planning a [spring stay](/en/spring) gets one extra advantage: you see the town at the precise moment it opens up.

## A little context: what a Czech spa town actually is

For first-time visitors it helps to know that Mariánské Lázně is not a wellness resort with a swimming pool attached. It is a working medical spa, part of the UNESCO-listed Great Spa Towns of Europe since 2021, where guests are examined by a spa physician and prescribed a course of treatments — mineral-water drinking cures, peat wraps, carbon dioxide baths — usually over one to three weeks. The parks, the promenades and the colonnade are not decoration around that: walking between the springs is itself part of the prescription, which is exactly why the town was laid out as one large garden.

## Parks designed by a single gardener

The scene people come for in spring is no accident. When the garden architect Václav Skalník arrived in 1817, he found a boggy valley full of ravines. He had it drained and filled, and laid out an English-style landscape park in its place, with the colonnade later rising at its centre. The gardens here are not an ornament beside the spa — they are its structural core, and they still look broadly as Skalník intended.

The main park below the colonnade carries his name. The Václav Skalník Dendrological Trail runs through the Skalník Gardens past more than 150 woody species, some of them up to two hundred years old — among them a snake-branch spruce ('Virgata'), a large-flowered oak and a tulip tree. In spring, when the crowns are still translucent, you can read the shape of a tree far better than in high summer.

The Úšovický brook runs through the gardens, so water is audible almost everywhere. Those damp margins are also where the spring snowflake grows — a specially protected species under Czech law, so it is one to admire rather than pick.

## The bloom calendar

At 630 metres above sea level, spring arrives later here than in the lowlands. Roughly, the rhythm goes like this:

- **March to early April — woodland ephemerals.** The forest floor goes first: spring snowflakes in damp ground and hollow corydalis, which finishes flowering before the trees come into leaf. The window is short and easily missed, but it is the quietest part of the season.
- **April — the town starts up.** The parks green over and seasonal venues reopen. The Royal Golf Club, the oldest course in the country, opened its 2026 season on 11 April.
- **Late April — the colonnade gets its soundtrack.** The Singing Fountain returns from its winter pause on 30 April.
- **Mid-May — peak bloom.** According to the town's official tourism portal, the ceremonial opening of the spa season takes place on the colonnade exactly when the rhododendrons and magnolias in the parks are in flower.

