## 30 April: the fountain comes back

The Singing Fountain in front of the Main Colonnade is the most reliable spring date in town. Built between 1982 and 1986, it played for the first time on 30 April 1986 — and every year since it is ceremonially switched on that same evening at 21:00, running until 31 October.

Through the season it plays daily from 07:00 on every odd hour, with the 21:00 and 22:00 performances lit in colour. The circular basin is eighteen metres across and holds a twelve-part stone flower with a polished stainless-steel centre; ten jet circuits are computer-controlled, so the combinations of water and music vary. There is more on the [history of the fountain](/en/magazine/singing-fountain) elsewhere on the site — though nothing quite replaces being there on the first spring evening it starts up again.

## The ceremonial opening of the spa season

The second great spring event is the opening of the spa season. It traditionally falls on the second weekend of May — in 2026 it ran from 8 to 10 May, with the main programme on the Saturday. It includes the ceremonial blessing and opening of the mineral springs, a procession in historical costume, a craft market, and promenade and colonnade concerts ranging from classical through folk to contemporary music.

The town usually announces the exact dates and programme for the coming year towards the end of winter, so if you want to plan a stay around that weekend, check the date on the town's official pages. Accommodation in the centre books up earlier for the season opening than at any other point in spring.

## Three spring walks from the colonnade

Spring is the best walking season here: no heat yet, dry paths, and views that reach further through bare crowns than they will in summer. Three routes starting near the colonnade:

**1. Skalník Gardens and the Dendrological Trail.** The shortest and gentlest option — a mild climb through the park from the roundabout up to the colonnade, past the pond, with labels on the notable trees. An hour at an unhurried pace, manageable even after a morning treatment.

**2. The Geological Park below Mlýnský vrch.** This nature trail, opened in 1988, runs about 1.5 kilometres along a beech-wooded slope above the City Museum, where it both begins and ends. Across ten hectares, 316 rock blocks are set out at 24 stops; the largest weighs sixteen tonnes. Their polished cut faces show the structure of the rocks that shaped this landscape and its springs — and because it runs through woodland, it is also the best place to catch the spring flowers. The Forest Spring (Lesní pramen) rises nearby, about 500 metres from the colonnade.

**3. Boheminium and the cable car.** The miniature park above the town is open all year, daily from 10:00 to 18:00 between September and June. The nicest approach is the cable car — running daily from 10:00 to 17:30 every fifteen minutes, with its upper station a hundred metres from the entrance gate. The ride crosses the greening valley and offers one of the few viewpoints where you see the whole of spring at once.

For longer routes, including trails into the Slavkovský les (Slavkov Forest) protected landscape area, see our roundup of the [finest walks](/en/magazine/best-walks) and the piece on [hiking trails around the town](/en/magazine/hiking-nature-trails-marienbad).

## The springs taste the same in spring

One thing the season does not change. Mariánské Lázně has around forty cold mineral springs within the town itself and roughly a hundred more in the wider area; these are acidulous waters at a steady 7 to 10 °C all year round. Come in April or come in August — the taste and the composition are identical. Only what surrounds you while you stand at the spout with your spa cup is different.

What should never be improvised, though, is the drinking cure. Which spring you drink and in what quantity is always determined individually by a spa physician according to your diagnosis; these are medicinal waters rather than table water, and several have a laxative effect at higher doses. The Cross Spring and Ferdinand Spring are traditionally associated with digestion and metabolism, the Rudolph Spring with kidneys and the urinary tract, and the Forest Spring with the upper respiratory tract. We cover them in detail in our [guide to the mineral springs](/en/magazine/mineral-springs-complete-guide). The initial medical consultation is part of every spa stay at the Ensana houses, so there is no need to book it separately.

## Practical notes for a spring stay

- **Layers and waterproof shoes.** At 630 metres, April and May can mean under five degrees in the morning and over fifteen by afternoon. Forest paths are often muddy after snowmelt and spring rain.
- **Mind the dates.** The mid-May season opening and the long weekends are the busiest; the first half of April is one of the calmest stretches of the whole year.
- **Expect a later spring.** What flowers in Prague in mid-April tends to arrive here two to three weeks later. If you are coming for the magnolias, aim at May rather than April.
- **Allergy sufferers.** The submontane air is clean, but birch and grass pollen reach Mariánské Lázně too — just somewhat later in the year.

Spring here has neither the melancholy of autumn nor the bustle of summer. It has something else: the sense that the whole town is starting up and you are there for it — and that for a few weeks the [surrounding countryside](/en/nature) makes a stronger impression than anything people built.
